# Process

## Tools used

I used an AI assistant to draft the first version of `fetch.py` and `plot.py`, and to suggest the overall structure. I then ran the scripts, read the output, and corrected the parts that did not match the actual USGS data.

## One thing I kept

I kept the colour-by-depth idea. The AI suggested using a `Normalize` object so that the full depth range maps onto the colour scale. That was a good suggestion because earthquake depths vary a lot, and a fixed colour scale would either wash out shallow quakes or hide deep ones. It also keeps the plotting code short.

## One thing I rejected

The AI first suggested using pandas to load and filter the GeoJSON. I rejected that. The GeoJSON is already a simple nested list of dictionaries, and the standard library `json` module is enough. Using pandas would add a large dependency and hide the loop over the features, which is part of what this assignment asks me to show.

## What I had to correct

The AI assumed every feature would have a magnitude and coordinates. The real feed contains rows where `geometry` is `null` or `mag` is missing. I added checks that skip those rows instead of crashing. I also removed an unused time-based plot that the AI suggested, because the assignment asks for one clear message per picture and the map already carries it.
